From 937992eadd4d6385a11760fe6847997198e01801 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 13:07:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08115/11362] [outer-loop] ARC action census: the
 74%-re-navigation figure is a tn36 property, not a roster property

Decomposes a live ARC exploration run's non-expanding actions into named classes on a
25-game, non-solve-conditioned corpus (E3AgentPolicy from a fresh reset on the offline
arcade, 240 actions per game, no LLM, no GPU).

Roster result (6000 actions, shipped config):
  34.40%  probe discovered a new state          (the work)
  34.40%  probe revisited a known state         (real move, known destination)
  19.13%  probe was inert -- frame unchanged    (largest AVOIDABLE class)
  11.65%  re-navigation (reset + replay + forward walk)
   0.42%  bootstrap reset

The premise correction: 73.8% re-navigation is tn36's number and tn36 is the MAXIMUM of
25 games (80.8% here). The median game spends 2.9%. Any lever sized off tn36 is sized
~6x too large.

Largest avoidable class -> zero is worth 1.53x on min((baseline/agent)^2, 115); all
re-navigation -> zero is 1.28x; both is 2.09x. Upper bounds, stated as such.

Mechanism probe (tier barrier off) shows the re-navigation that DOES exist is caused by
the global tier barrier's departure rule, not by reset-only env semantics: roster
navigation halves and tn36 collapses 194 -> 2 actions. Not a recommendation -- the same
arm cuts new-state discovery 9.3% roster-wide, which is what the barrier was shipped to
buy.

Measured nulls: frontier locality ordering is worth ~0 (depth-primary already picks the
cheapest reachable node); forward-walk navigation already dominates reset-replay 147/270
episodes; raising frontier_batch_size does NOT fan out at the target (only index 0 gets
`origin`), so the 462-action amortization ceiling is not reachable through that flag.

Nothing under python/carnot/agentic/ was modified; the instrument only observes between
decisions. adversarial_verify: 10 artifacts, 0 flagged.

